# Sony PlayStation 4 network device configuration
#

config NET_VENDOR_SONY
	bool "Sony devices"
	default y
	depends on PCI
	help
	  If you have a network (Ethernet) device belonging to this class,
	  say Y.

	  Note that the answer to this question doesn't directly affect the
	  kernel: saying N will just cause the configurator to skip all the
	  questions about Sony devices. If you say Y, you will be asked for
	  your specific device in the following questions.

if NET_VENDOR_SONY

config PS4_GBE
	tristate "PlayStation 4 Baikal gigabit Ethernet support"
	depends on PCI && X86_64
	depends on X86_PS4
	select CRC32
	help
	  Driver for the gigabit Ethernet controller in the PlayStation 4
	  Slim's Baikal southbridge, PCI function 00:14.1, device ID
	  104d:90d8. Written from a hardware specification recovered by
	  reverse engineering, published at
	  https://github.com/Gcsman110/ps4-mts-linux-driver

	  The PHY is a MediaTek MT7531-family GPHY on the same die, reached
	  through the controller's own MDIO window.

	  This driver needs the Baikal PCIe glue driver (drivers/ps4) for
	  per-part EFUSE calibration, so PS4 must be enabled.

	  Say Y here to build it into the kernel, which is usually what you
	  want: the root filesystem is frequently on the network this device
	  provides, so loading it as a module can be too late to be useful.

	  To build it as a module choose M; the module will be called
	  ps4_gbe.

endif # NET_VENDOR_SONY
